{
  "request": {
    "model": "nomic-embed-text:latest",
    "input": [
      "Synthetic nurse note for software testing. No real patient data.\n\nDuring a commercial-driver review after a refrigerated-freight run, the nurse brings together the driver's account, a dated clinical attachment and an employer form copied from an earlier assessment; the documents refer to different times, and the current entry distinguishes a completed examination from recollections, plans and information concerning another person.\n\nThe current ophthalmologist confirms monocular vision following a remote injury and measures visual acuity of 6/9 in the remaining eye, with a horizontal field of 145 degrees measured within 10 degrees above and below the horizontal midline; the signed specialist report explicitly finds no other significant field loss likely to impede driving, whereas an old binocular screening entry describes the driver's condition before the injury and is retained only as historical context.\n\nThe interview takes place before the driver's next planned roster, and the nurse records changes in symptoms, treatment and available documents without assuming that a future specialist appointment has already occurred; any letter that has been requested but not supplied remains separate from the findings actually available when this occupational record is written.\n\nFor the occupational reviewer, the nurse retains both the definite findings and the limitations of the available account, including the timing and testing conditions where they matter, rather than producing a single reassuring label; the driver is told which information is on file and which details still require confirmation, without replacing the recorded clinical evidence with an employer's preference.\n"
    ],
    "truncate": false
  },
  "provider_called": true,
  "status": "completed",
